MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C: <EDH>
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_001: Say, Esme... You're really from some fishing village?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_002: Sure am! Fisher's daughter, born and bred. Why do\nyou ask?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_003: Because on the battlefield, you're something else! So\nreliable. To think you grew up gutting fish...
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_004: Oh! I see what you mean. But compared to you, I'm\nnothing special.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_005: I mean, reliable? You've got all this experience... And\nyou were famous, right? A famous mercenary!
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_006: I was. In fact, now that you ask, I might as well tell\nthe whole tale.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_007: Truth is, I used to raise hell out there. By the time I\nwas your age, I'd already bagged a general's head.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_008: Are you kidding? That's wild! If you were so good...\nwhy did you quit?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_009: Well, I got pregnant. When Mu came along, I moved\nback home.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_010: You might remember her, actually. She's in Leda's\ngroup these days.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_011: With Mu to raise, I retired from fighting. For 14\nwhole years, in fact.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_012: From the time Mu was born till recently—14\nyears—I focused on making a home for her.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_013: That long? Could have fooled me. I saw the way you\nblocked that sword. Didn't even break a sweat!
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_014: Well, it's not like I ever stopped training. But come\non! That guy was so weak it barely counts.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_015: OK, so... You took a break from fighting. But how did\nyou end up in that tavern where we met?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_016: Just trying to pick up work. I need money so Mu can\nstudy and one day serve at Smyrnos's temple.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_017: If she's going to do that, she needs to apprentice with\na famous mage in Arago.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_018: It's not an easy path. Or a cheap one. But I want to\nget her on that road. She'll be a great mage.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_019: OK, that makes sense...but can you really make\nmoney by fighting in the Heroic Games?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_020: Well, I have two reasons to fight. First is that I owe\nyou for that day.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_021: The second is, my edge has dulled. I'm a mercenary,\nand I want to get back in the game.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_022: I'm no use at anything else. Seems like I need a little\ndanger in my life.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_023: You really put your life on the line for your daughter!\n{SetColor=Gray}I wonder how my mom is doing...{SetColor=Default}
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_024: Esme? What's wrong?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_025: Oh, nothing! Come on—let's get training!
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_026: After all, you need your edge back, and I can help!
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_027: You're a kind girl. Almost feels like I have two\ndaughters now.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_028: If you find yourself missing your mom, come find me.\nI'll give you a big bear hug, tell you everything's OK.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_029: Uh... Thanks, but I think I'm fine.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_C_030: Ahaha. I guess that makes sense. It's your own\nmom or nothing, right?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B: <EDH>
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_001: Hey, Mikaela, what's up? You said you wanted\nto talk. Something wrong?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_002: Fabio spilled the beans... Is it true that you and your\nmom don't get along?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_003: Me and my big mouth... I talked about Mu, then\npretended to be a mom to you.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_004: I probably just opened up old wounds with all that.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_005: Relax. You're fine. Fabio got it wrong—I don't hate\nmy mom or anything.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_006: She was just taken in by that shaman. She's very\ndevout, so it's not a surprise.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_007: I see... You're a good kid. Did you talk to her before\nyou left?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_008: Not really. And nothing's changed since then. She's...\nShe's probably relieved I left.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_009: Thing is, my dad and I both have the godmark.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_010: But whatever god it is doesn't exist in Dagda.\nThe villagers called us devils.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_011: That scared my mom, and things only got worse\nwhen Dad went missing.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_012: Do you really think she's doing better with you gone?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_013: Uhh... She must be, right?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_014: Still, send her a letter. You don't know what she's\nreally thinking.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_015: You're strong. Fate marked you for death, and you\nfought back! Why hesitate now?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_016: Thanks, Mikaela. You're right. I should have talked\nto you sooner.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_017: No thanks needed. I'm just a busybody, like any mom\nof a teenage girl.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_018: Please, stick your nose right in my business. Mu's\nlucky to have you!
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_019: You say that, but I'm a terrible mother. Maybe\nthe worst.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_020: The worst? How can you say that? Every day, you\nrisk your life to earn money for her!
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_021: I'm just paying back what my husband squandered.\nWhen it came to dads, I didn't pick a winner.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_022: That's not your fault! He was the one who wasted\nthat money, not you.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_023: Well... Remember what I said about work? How I'm\nno use?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_024: Before I met you, I worked in a mine. I left Mu with\nmy sister and worked like a dog for six months.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_025: But I ended up with zero. I got scammed. All\nthat work...
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_026: I'd promised to send money, and when it didn't turn\nup, Mu ran off.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_027: Oh no... Have you had a chance to talk to her\nabout it?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_028: About letting some scoundrels get the better of me?\nI can't. It's too embarrassing.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_B_029: But I will speak with her. Once I get the money back,\nI'll give it to her, and we'll have a talk.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A: <EDH>
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_001: Sounds like mom's taking care of all the village kids!\nThat's probably a lot of work...but a lot of fun too!
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_002: She's still sending you letters? I'm glad you two\nmade up.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_003: You were right, of course. Sending her a letter was a\ngreat idea. She was worried about me.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_004: What did I tell you? That's just how it is with moms\nand their daughters. This makes me so happy.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_005: Good. Because it's your turn!
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_006: What? No! I don't have the money.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_007: I had a feeling you might say that... Ahem...\n"PS Tell Mikaela that she's one smart fish cake."
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_008: Wait just a second—
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_009: "But you have to get her to talk to Mu. They're both\nsuffering in silence."
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_010: That's what Mom had to say. Now, what do you have\nto say?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_011: Are you kidding? You talked to your mom\nabout me?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_012: Well, obviously! We're friends, and what you said\nmeant a lot.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_013: Sure, fine... But you didn't have to tell her about Mu!
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_014: Oh, shush. Let me explain.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_015: Mom told me that whenever she saw me with my\ngodmark, she'd get scared I'd get eaten by a devil.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_016: And when my dad disappeared, she was sure that's\nwhat happened to him. It terrified her.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_017: So why didn't she tell you that?
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_018: She was too scared to say it out loud. If she said it\nout loud, it might happen, so she kept mum.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_019: I wish she'd told me. I would have laughed so hard.\nInstead, she worried about it day in, day out.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_020: Oh...
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_021: So now you know why I told her about you and Mu.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_022: It's the same thing, right? Mom loves daughter, won't\ntalk to daughter, sits around and worries.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_023: You have to talk to Mu. No, not talk—listen to her.\nReally listen. And then respond.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_024: If Mu's waiting for something from you, I don't think\nit's money.
MID_SE_Esm_Mic_A_025: You know what? You're right. I owe it to her.